 2-Wasserstein / energy distance, mutual-information overlap, histogram L1, or a combination) and why; (d) the control and null-model strategy (e.g., comparing MD-to-MD as a positive control, a randomized or untrained model as a negative control, permutation tests); (e) the analysis pipeline from raw trajectory to summary statistic, including any preprocessing (clustering, lag-time choice for TICA, windowing, block averaging for uncertainty); (f) a feasibility paragraph noting what is computationally tractable for tetrapeptide all-atom trajectories at typical lengths. - For the tetrapeptide system: the context says 'tetrapeptides simulated via all-atom MD.' Please state explicitly which sequence or set of sequences you will assume, or flag this as an open assumption if the source does not specify. Do not invent a specific sequence and present it as fact. ITEM 3 — CONSEQUENTIAL ASSUMPTIONS AND UNCERTAINTIES: - List every assumption your proposed approach rests on that is NOT confirmed by the source material. For example: the identity of the forward simulation model (the question says 'forward simulation model' but does not name it); the number of TICA components; the MD engine and force field used for ground-truth trajectories; trajectory length and number of replicas; whether the forward model is a Markov model, a generative model, or something else. - For each assumption, state what would change in the design if the assumption were wrong. I want to see the sensitivity of the design to these unknowns before we lock in the approach. Please do not proceed to write the full design document yet. I want to review your proposed structure and flag assumptions at approach review before the main work begins. Accuracy first, as always — if something is not in the source, say so rather than filling the gap.", "required_handoff_stage": "plan", "approach_approved": false, "public_task": {"question": "Do the sampled trajectories from the forward simulation model exhibit distribut